# How Taliesin works {.unnumbered}

You change one number in a `.tmd` file and hit save. The paragraph you touched
re-renders in place, and nothing else moves: your scroll position holds, the 3D
canvas two sections down keeps spinning, the Jupyter kernel stays warm. No flash,
no reload, no cold start. **This book is how that works.**

The companion [User Guide](../guide/) is about *using* Taliesin; this one is about
*how it is built*, for contributors, for anyone extending it, and for the curious
reader who would rather see the design decisions than take them on faith.

## The one idea

Everything here follows from a single architectural choice: **all of the
intelligence lives in a Rust core, and every client is thin.** The core parses a
`.tmd` into a block model, renders each block to HTML, and speaks a small, stable
websocket protocol. The browser preview, the `build` command, and the VS Code companion
extension are all just consumers of that protocol; the next client is a new consumer, not
a rewrite. Nothing clever lives in the client.

Three properties fall out of that design, and they are what the rest of this book
explains in detail:

- **Click-to-source.** Every block carries the source position it came from, so the
  preview can map any rendered element back to its line, even across included files.
- **Block-level incremental updates.** A save diffs the new block model against the
  old and ships only the differences, so live state (scroll position, a spinning 3D
  canvas, a warm kernel) survives an edit.
- **No per-edit startup cost.** A warm server and a warm Jupyter kernel mean a save
  re-runs only the code that actually changed.

## Our running example

To keep things concrete, the chapters follow one tiny document, `post.tmd`:

````markdown
# Cooling coffee

A fresh cup cools toward room temperature, the gap shrinking exponentially:
$T(t) = T_\text{room} + (T_0 - T_\text{room})\,e^{-t/\tau}$.

```{python}
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

t = np.linspace(0, 30, 200)
plt.plot(t, 20 + 70 * np.exp(-t / 10))   # tau = 10 minutes
plt.show()
```
````

Three blocks: a heading, a paragraph with a line of math, and a Python cell that
draws a cooling curve. It is barely a document, yet it touches every part of the
system, prose and a heading to render, math to typeset, code to highlight and to
*run*, and an output to splice back in. Across the book we watch it become blocks,
get diffed, cached, and pushed to the browser. When a chapter needs a concrete
edit, we turn one knob: the cooling constant `tau`, from 10 to 12.

And because this book is itself built with Taliesin, that cell is not a screenshot.
The curve below is drawn by running `post.tmd`'s code right here, as the page
renders, the very pipeline the rest of the book describes:

```{python}
#| echo: false
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

t = np.linspace(0, 30, 200)
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.2), tight_layout=True)
ax.plot(t, 20 + 70 * np.exp(-t / 10), lw=2, color="#5b8cff")  # tau = 10 minutes
ax.set_xlabel("minutes")
ax.set_ylabel("temperature (°C)")
for spine in ("top", "right"):
    ax.spines[spine].set_visible(False)
plt.show()
```

## How to read this book

The chapters build on each other, roughly from the outside in:

- **[The repository](repository.tmd)** is the lay of the land: what every top-level
  directory is for, so you know where to look before opening a file.
- **[Architecture](architecture.tmd)** is the map: the crates, the data flow, and
  what happens on a save.
- **[The rendering pipeline](rendering.tmd)** follows a `.tmd` from raw text to a
  block model to HTML.
- **[Offline-first rendering & theming](offline-theming.tmd)** is how math, code, and
  themes render server-side and offline, so a light/dark toggle re-styles the page
  with nothing to re-render.
- **[The multi-page subsystem](sites.tmd)** is how a directory becomes a website or a
  book: discovery, chrome, listings, cross-page references, and search.
- **[The block model](block-model.tmd)** is the load-bearing data structure, and the
  websocket message contract that rides on it.
- **[Code execution & the kernel](execution.tmd)** covers the warm Jupyter kernel,
  the execution plan, and the persistent output cache.
- **[The dev server](server.tmd)** is the long-running process: the file watcher, the
  websocket, and the multi-page site server.
- **[Validation & schemas](validation.tmd)** is the closed-vocabulary validator, the
  located warnings it emits, and the drift-locked JSON Schemas behind editor autocomplete.
- **[Extending Taliesin](extending.tmd)** is the conventions for adding a feature, and
  where extensions live outside the core.

If you read only one chapter, read [Architecture](architecture.tmd). It frames
everything else.
